00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.042
And then to get stuff from
your machine to your VM,

00:10:45.042 --> 00:10:46.500
to get the code on
it, you're going

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:50.970
to want to run the gcloud
compute sep, and then source

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.440
and destination, just like
you would with SEP normally.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.691
So with these three commands,
I can do everything I need,

00:10:59.691 --> 00:11:00.190
right?

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:01.890
I can build a machine.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.680
I can install all the proper
software I need on it.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.752
And then I can push my
application code to it.

00:11:06.752 --> 00:11:08.460
Now, obviously, there
are different way--

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.910
you can do all this differently,
but this just is bare bones,

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:12.930
like let's just--

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.580
the easiest way to get it done.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:16.660
We can do this.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.530
So I don't like to
talk about price,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.910
but I feel like with
VMs and our technology,

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.920
price is an important
differentiator.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.920
So I want to talk
about it a little bit.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.740
I promise I won't
get too salesy.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.750
So one of the
things that we do is

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:35.160
when you start configuring
machines, up in the right

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.850
half side of the screen,
you see an estimate, right?

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:41.790
And as I add resources,
I add more processors,

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.716
the cost per month goes up.

00:11:43.716 --> 00:11:44.340
And it goes up.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.080
And I'll go, I
went crazy, right?

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.960
Because Google pays for
these when I spin them up,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.570
so I like to make them big.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:52.981
And you'll see
I've got a charge.

00:11:52.981 --> 00:11:55.230
Now, when you break down the
charge, one of the things

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.150
that we do, and it's
really important to know,

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:58.590
is that we have a base
charge if you're just

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.570
running your machine
kind of haphazardly.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:03.520
But if you run your machine for
a consistent period of time,

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.290
up to a month--

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.190
a month being 100% of
the time you need to run,

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.398
and then the next month you
get another sustained use

00:12:11.398 --> 00:12:13.890
discount-- we will knock
a significant portion

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:16.800
off the price,
because we appreciate

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.104
having consistent load size.

00:12:18.104 --> 00:12:19.770
We appreciate being
able to plan better.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:23.190
Because you have a set
size that you're going

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.960
to keep running in perpetuity.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.414
Now, this is great.

00:12:27.414 --> 00:12:28.830
Sustainable use
discount is great.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.930
And like for things you
want running all the time,

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:31.638
it's really good.

00:12:31.638 --> 00:12:33.827
But there's another option
we should talk about,

00:12:33.827 --> 00:12:35.160
which is called preemptable VMs.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.700
And you'll notice that's
significantly less.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.110
But there's a catch, right?

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.940
Because there's
got to be a catch.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:47.954
The catch is that a
preemptable VM, which

00:12:47.954 --> 00:12:50.370
is what-- you have to flip a
little setting and it becomes

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:51.510
preemptable.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.600
Preventable VMs will disappear
sometime in the next 24 hours.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.762
And they're guaranteed
to go away in 24 hours.

00:12:56.762 --> 00:12:57.970
But they can go away anytime.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:01.070
So I have literally
started up a preemptable VM

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.090
and then went to SSH
into it and it's gone.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.974
And I was completely confused.

00:13:05.974 --> 00:13:07.140
So why would you want these?

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.780
Well, a lot of
times, we have work

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.580
that we need to do kind
of whenever it gets

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.310
done batch work, big data jobs.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:17.487
Well, you can push those jobs.

00:13:17.487 --> 00:13:19.570
As long as you have some
sort of queue set up that

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.740
can be tolerant of a
machine going down,

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:25.530
you can do them for 1/5
the cost of the normal way

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.530
of doing that operation.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:32.966
As long as soon as the machine
goes down, the queue holds,

00:13:32.966 --> 00:13:34.590
you can always request
another machine.

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:36.423
There's nothing stopping
you from requesting

00:13:36.423 --> 00:13:37.600
another preemptable machine.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.990
And then boom, you're
going through your queue

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.542
at a pretty good rate, and
doing it at 1/5 the cost.

00:13:42.542 --> 00:13:45.000
Or you could just spin up five
times the number of machines

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.610
and get it done in
1/5 the time, I think.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:51.090
My math is a little
bit hokey there.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:53.760
All right, so I talked
about disk images.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.610
We provide disk images, but
you can create your own.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.830
Typically, what I do is
start with one of our images,

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.910
modify, customize, make
it unique to what I want,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:04.194
and then freeze it.

00:14:04.194 --> 00:14:05.610
And then I can
create new machines

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.436
from that, which
is really helpful.

00:14:07.436 --> 00:14:09.060
But I could also make
them from scratch

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.351
using virtual bots in a
process that I have never done.

00:14:11.351 --> 00:14:17.260
So good luck, but
it can be done.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.300
IP addresses-- you can
get private and public IP

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:20.630
addresses.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.990
You can definitely turn
off public IP addresses.

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:24.790
You can also get
static or ephemeral.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:26.830
One of the things I like is
when you get an ephemeral IP

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:28.870
address from us, if
something happens and someone

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:30.190
shares that
ephemeral IP address,

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:32.500
and now that's the permanent
IP address of the install,

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:35.037
and you're like but this
could go away anytime,

00:14:35.037 --> 00:14:37.120
you can make any one of
our ephemeral IP addresses

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:37.760
just static.

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:40.850
You can just say, give me
that, and that's mine now,

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:41.954
which is great.

00:14:41.954 --> 00:14:43.370
Or you can just
have a static one.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:44.745
Now, they don't
cost you anything

00:14:44.745 --> 00:14:48.820
unless you take a static
one and then park on it.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:51.411
Because we're all trying to
avoid the IP4 apocalypse,

00:14:51.411 --> 00:14:51.910
right?

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:55.000
So we want to make sure
that if you have addresses,

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.020
you're using them.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:59.060
Firewall-- briefly
talked about that.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.430
You create a rule.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:05.610
Open port 3306 on TCP.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.640
And then you give that
rule a tag, a name.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.740
So that would be
MySQL, for those

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.510
who are play along at home.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:14.660
And then you tag machines
with those rules.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:17.650
And that's how you
open up the firewall.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.240
We'll take a slight detour
and talk about Cloud Shell.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:21.400
I really love Cloud Shell.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:22.710
It's a great developer tool.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.141
It's a really great
way of not having

00:15:25.141 --> 00:15:27.140
to have this stuff on
your machine all the time.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:30.099
I didn't show it, but there's
actually a code editor

00:15:30.099 --> 00:15:31.640
that you can use,
so you can actually

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:36.500
add code on the fly
in reasonable syntax

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.150
highlighting everything.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.430
Really helpful when
you're in a pinch,

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.290
when people are calling
you and you are not

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:46.180
near your computer, or
your own personal computer.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.650
All right, we're back.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:49.960
There's one last consideration
I want to talk about.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:53.950
I'm talking about our VMs and
our network and our setup.

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:57.560
When you create one of
our VMs on our network,

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:01.360
you don't have to do any
setup to make one data center

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:02.860
talk to another.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:05.410
By default, in one
project, all your machines

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:06.460
talk to one another.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:10.640
And they use our really, really
high powered backend network

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:12.110
to do that.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:14.620
And so instead of having
to go to a different zone

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:17.140
and find, oh, this data
center has these machines,

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.290
and then this data has--

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:19.790
they're all in one project.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:22.390
And so this means you
can do crazy, fun things,

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:23.840
especially with our network.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:26.200
So I'm going to
switch over to a demo.

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:28.930
And I want to take a
selfie, if that's OK.

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:31.750
I'm going to take a
selfie with all of you.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.890
I hope that's OK.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:36.600
If it's not, we'll blur
out faces eventually.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:37.510
That's OK, no one's--

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:39.100
all right, so here we are.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:43.780
We're going to-- and boom.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:44.930
Yeah, all right.

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:50.630
And so let me--

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.760
this was the test, right?

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:55.100
I am clearly not
pleased with any of you.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:59.595
No, I'll upload this
and I'll refresh.

00:16:59.595 --> 00:17:01.700
Oh, come on.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:02.660
Oh, I didn't upload.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.201
You actually have
to upload, like you

00:17:04.201 --> 00:17:05.390
have to press the button.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:06.260
So here we go.

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:07.810
We're uploading.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:09.510
There we go.

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:10.584
There-- oh.

00:17:10.584 --> 00:17:12.750
All right, you know what,
I'm going to roll with it.

00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:17.450
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to take this picture,

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.369
and you'll see that
I have this map.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:22.057
I have machines in all
of these data centers.

00:17:22.057 --> 00:17:24.140
And I set them up really
easy, just with a script,

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:25.260
just like I showed you.

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:26.480
And what I'm going to do is
I'm going to take this picture

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.050
and bounce it
around our network.

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:30.862
And every time it hits a
different point on the network,

00:17:30.862 --> 00:17:33.320
we're going to stamp it as if
it had like a little passport

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:34.010
stamp.

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.329
And you can see it happening
in live, real time.

00:17:36.329 --> 00:17:37.370
We're actually traveling.

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:38.990
So we went from Taiwan
to Tokyo to Sydney

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.555
to Oregon to Iowa to Virginia
to London to Frankfurt and Sao

00:17:41.555 --> 00:17:44.700
Paulo faster than I
could say it out loud.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:49.910
We did it in 4.4 seconds
with 600 milliseconds or so

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:50.912
of processing.

00:17:50.912 --> 00:17:52.370
What's great about
this is I didn't

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:55.710
have to do anything with the
network to get this to work.

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:58.040
It just worked out of the box.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:00.620
And that's something
that I really like--

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:04.610
having tried other providers,
I get spoiled with this,

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:07.131
like not having to do a
tremendous amount of work,

00:18:07.131 --> 00:18:09.380
which I shouldn't really say
that I'm happy with that.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.338
My boss is in the room.

00:18:10.338 --> 00:18:12.020
But it's OK.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:13.670
I do like working.

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:16.610
I just-- I don't like
wiring up networking.

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.715
With that, I'm going
to switch to the preso,

00:18:19.715 --> 00:18:21.840
and we're going to move on
to the next topic, which

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:22.339
is storage.

00:18:22.339 --> 00:18:24.300
Where do I put my stuff?

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.390
So at the file
storage level, we have

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:30.350
two products, one which
we announced recently,

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:31.100
and Cloud Storage.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:33.308
I'm going to talk through
both of those options, talk

00:18:33.308 --> 00:18:34.760
why you'd choose
one or the other.

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:36.980
So Cloud Storage is
bucket-based storage.

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:40.349
We didn't-- everybody
does bucket-based storage.

00:18:40.349 --> 00:18:41.640
It's kind of a common metaphor.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.120
We didn't steal it from anybody.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:45.830
With one set of
interfaces, even though I

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:46.970
have all these
different types I'm

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:48.830
going to talk about, every
time you deal with them,

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.496
whether you're going
through the console

00:18:50.496 --> 00:18:55.440
or you're going through
CLI tools or SDKs,

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:57.680
it's the same commands,
just with different types

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.359
of storage.

00:18:58.359 --> 00:18:59.650
What are those different types?

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:01.483
Well, we'll start with
multi-regional, which

00:19:01.483 --> 00:19:02.930
is globally available storage.

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:05.096
This is for stuff that you
want to push out as close

00:19:05.096 --> 00:19:06.740
to your users as possible.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:09.410
You want it to be for
web content, or videos,

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:10.220
or mobile stuff.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.440
You want it to be that when
your users go to get it,

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:14.900
it is as close to
them as possible.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:16.945
As opposed to regional,
where what you really

00:19:16.945 --> 00:19:18.320
are trying to do
is you're trying

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.780
to keep it as close to the
processing as possible.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.660
Like this is big data
jobs, or maybe you

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:26.420
need to stay in certain
regions of the world.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:27.950
You data, you can't leave it.

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:30.170
Or other reasons.

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:33.590
But here, the data
is only in one region

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:36.380
and it's close to the machines
you're using to process it.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:39.080
Now, this doesn't mean that
you can't open up regional data

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:42.590
to the rest of the world,
like someone in California

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:45.080
can request something in
the European data center.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.975
No, you totally can,
it's just the latency

00:19:46.975 --> 00:19:49.100
is going to be a little
bit longer because it's not

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:52.060
duplicated as close to
the user as possible.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.440
Then we have two other
options, nearline and coldline.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.420
Both are longer term
storage options.

00:19:56.420 --> 00:19:59.570
Nearline are for backups
and long tail media.

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:03.350
There's stuff that you
occasionally get to.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.930
And it's planned that you'd
have regular access to it,

00:20:05.930 --> 00:20:08.690
but it's not something that
you're getting every day.

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:10.940
And then coldline,
I like to say,

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:15.410
when you're downloading stuff
from coldline in one browser

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:17.787
window, you have your
resume open in another.

00:20:17.787 --> 00:20:20.120
Because it's for compliance
and disaster recovery stuff.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.297
Stuff that you hope
you never have to pull.

00:20:22.297 --> 00:20:24.630
So you want to store that,
because you have to store it,

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:25.820
but you want to store it
as cheaply as possible

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.360
and never ever use it.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.562
So that's what coldline is for.

00:20:30.562 --> 00:20:34.130
This is kind of the
general cost in the US.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:37.200
And they're different
in different regions,

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.530
but the deltas are always
pretty much the same.

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:43.490
Multi-regional is $0.026
cents per gigabyte per month.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.450
Regional is $0.02 per
gigabyte per month.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:47.370
Nearline is $0.01 per
gigabyte per month.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:49.080
And then coldline
is the cheapest

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.140
at $0.007 per
gigabyte per month.

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:57.870
There's also another cost
involved, which is retrieval.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:00.080
So if I go and retrieve a
file from multi-regional or

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.152
regional, we don't charge you
for just accessing the file.

00:21:03.152 --> 00:21:04.610
Whereas with nearline
and coldline,

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:07.234
because we want to encourage you
to kind of park them and leave

00:21:07.234 --> 00:21:10.880
them, we have a $0.01 per
gigabyte charge for pulling

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:13.489
down from nearline, and a
$0.05 per gigabyte charge

00:21:13.489 --> 00:21:14.780
for pulling down from coldline.

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:16.821
Again, that's probably
the least of your problems

00:21:16.821 --> 00:21:19.324
then, but that's just now.

00:21:19.324 --> 00:21:20.740
All right, we have
another option,

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.200
which is called Cloud
Filestore, which we, again,

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:24.390
released recently.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:27.869
It's basically managed NFS,
and it's a shared file system.

00:21:27.869 --> 00:21:29.910
You get really low latency
because you connect it

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:33.267
to the machines you're actually
using, usually in the same data

00:21:33.267 --> 00:21:34.070
center.

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.360
There are two service tiers,
a premium and a standard.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.820
It's basically-- it's NFS.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:40.350
It's NFS that we manage.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:43.690
So what should we use?

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:45.360
Well, NFS is
attractive, especially

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.280
if I'm using it
somewhere inside,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:49.290
but the fact of the
matter is, I want to share

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:50.547
this data with the world.

00:21:50.547 --> 00:21:52.380
I don't want to do a
lot of work to do that.

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:55.980
If I do NFS, then I have
to put some sort of system

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:56.897
to share on top of it.

00:21:56.897 --> 00:21:57.979
And I have to manage that.

00:21:57.979 --> 00:21:59.910
With Cloud Storage, as
you'll see in a second,

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:01.980
it just shares immediately
and it's pretty easy.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.396
So I'm going to
use Cloud Storage.

00:22:03.396 --> 00:22:06.240
So let me give you a quick
demo of Cloud Storage.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.980
I'm going to switch
to the laptop again.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:11.119
And here we go.

00:22:11.119 --> 00:22:12.160
I'm on the laptop, great.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:14.530
I'm going to go ahead
and create a bucket.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:18.520
I'm going to call it tagger 102.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.120
I'm just going
create a default one.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:22.332
And you'll see that's it.

00:22:22.332 --> 00:22:23.290
That's all I had to do.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:24.210
It's already created.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:27.010
I'm actually going to
upload files already.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:31.870
So let's just upload all
the files from our app.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:34.830
You'll see that they push
up, and they're going fast.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:35.880
Fantastic.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:37.597
Now they're in Cloud Storage.

00:22:37.597 --> 00:22:39.430
And one of the things
I love about this is--

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:41.759
so let's see, Cloud
Storage, Gladiator.

00:22:41.759 --> 00:22:43.050
I'm going to share it publicly.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:46.044
And when I do that, I click
Public Link, boom, public.

00:22:46.044 --> 00:22:46.710
That's how fast.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:47.940
It's like already there.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.680
You see I've got a URL for it.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.069
Now, one of the
other files is Ewok.

00:22:52.069 --> 00:22:53.610
I'm going to pick
because it's short.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:57.000
And I'm going to do a
little URL walk in here.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.880
Ooh, I did not mean that
pun and it came out.

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:00.870
Oh, no.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.030
It's terrible.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.040
And you'll see that
I can URL walk,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:09.090
and I don't get it, because
those types of permission

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:11.660
sharing happen that fast.

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:14.460
So Cloud Storage is
sort of drop dead easy.

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:16.740
Like it just boom, works.

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:22.129
So with that, I'm going to
switch back to presentation.

00:23:22.129 --> 00:23:24.170
All right, so you get
stuff to the Cloud Storage.

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.670
You can do it via
command line using

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:28.650
gsutil, which is equivalent to
gcloud, but for file storage

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:30.400
operations.

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:34.170
I can share publicly
by using gsutili iam

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:35.310
to do those operations.

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:38.670
Now, one thing I'll point
out is that I shared one file

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.650
within a private bucket.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:42.690
I don't recommend doing
that, because that's

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:43.657
when bad things happen.

00:23:43.657 --> 00:23:45.240
That's when human
error will take over

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:47.760
and you'll accidentally share
something you don't want to.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:49.920
So what I like to do is
I have public buckets

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.110
and private buckets and
I don't mix the two.

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:53.750
But your mileage may vary.

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:56.490
Do what you will,
but that's my advice.

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.850
So my app needs to
talk to these things.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:03.536
And to talk to the app, I
need use our client libraries.

00:24:03.536 --> 00:24:05.160
Now, our client
libraries, you see here

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:07.090
the languages that are listed.

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:11.790
We also have REST APIs that
are reasonably well documented

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:14.550
if you need to do it
from another language.

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:16.800
But I'm going to use
PHP, because that's

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:18.300
what I used to
write this app and I

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:19.740
wanted to get it done fast.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:22.350
Well, when you use PHP to move
files that have been uploaded,

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.570
you use move uploaded file,
because it's right on the nose.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:27.315
That's the system
command to do it.

00:24:27.315 --> 00:24:29.190
And you see, I take a
source and destination.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.310
Now, to do this
using the SDK, I need

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:34.500
to do a little bit more code.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:36.660
I need to use the right library.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.760
I need to create
a storage client,

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:41.010
create a storage
bucket reference,

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:42.480
and then upload the file.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:46.380
But not a tremendous amount
of more work in order

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:48.234
to accomplish the same thing.

00:24:48.234 --> 00:24:49.650
So basically, I
just swap that in.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:52.470
And then our app is
looking like this now.

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:55.247
I've moved all the images
to a cloud-based solution.

00:24:55.247 --> 00:24:57.580
The application code is still
living on the same machine

00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:58.560
as the database.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:00.330
And we should fix that.

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.570
So let's go next and talk
about application storage.

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:06.730
Clearly, we get here, we get
the whole SQL versus NoSQL

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:07.410
conundrum.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:12.450
I-- oh, my phone is going
off because I am dumb.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:15.640
So SQL versus NoSQL, you
get in this conundrum.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.430
And I don't clearly
have a winner,

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:19.120
although I do hate one of them.

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:22.072
But I won't tell you which.

00:25:22.072 --> 00:25:23.030
Both of them are valid.

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:24.080
Both of them do things.

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:27.520
So let's talk about SQL first.

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:30.690
So Cloud SQL is
our tool for SQL.

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.040
It is a traditional SQL Server.

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:34.090
It is vertically scalable.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:37.650
We provide managed backups
and easy to set up replicas.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.100
And we make it easy to
do high availability.

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.320
Vertically scalable means
you can only get it so big.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:45.296
And then if you
need more resources,

00:25:45.296 --> 00:25:46.670
you need a more
powerful machine,

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:49.060
you have to come up with
some other solution.

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:50.910
We also have something
called Cloud Spanner,

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.040
which is for structured data.

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:54.000
It's SQL.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.310
It's both highly available
and strongly consistent.

00:25:56.310 --> 00:26:00.300
Now, those of you that know
CAP theorem may say that's

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:01.860
kind of impossible
in CAP theorem,

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.599
and databases, like
what are you doing here?

00:26:04.599 --> 00:26:07.140
So what we did is we found the
guy who wrote the CAP theorem.

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.910
We hired him, made him a VP.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:12.780
And he's kind of like,
uh, you could say it.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:14.949
'Tis sort of close to the truth.

00:26:14.949 --> 00:26:16.990
Basically, there are
reasons why we can say that.

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:19.540
There's a big white
paper, you can read it.

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:21.890
It is both of these
things and it is amazing.

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:24.550
It's globally distributed
and horizontally scalable,

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:26.357
which means you can
keep adding stuff.

00:26:26.357 --> 00:26:27.940
If you need resources
in other places,

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:29.356
you can add them
in another place.

00:26:29.356 --> 00:26:32.500
It's important to note that
this is cost effective at scale

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.150
for this app.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:36.899
It would be like massive
overkill and my boss

00:26:36.899 --> 00:26:38.440
would fire me,
because my bills would

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:42.320
be much higher than the real
value of Tagger the app, right?

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:46.310
So make sure that it's cost
effective for you to use it.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:49.885
But if you have that type
of scale, it is amazing.

00:26:49.885 --> 00:26:51.660
Let's talk about
our NoSQL option.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:53.820
The two main ones that I
like, that I use a lot,

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.611
and I would use in this
sort of application

00:26:55.611 --> 00:26:58.410
is Cloud Datastore
or Cloud Firestore.

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.060
Both are document-based.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:01.320
Both are indexable.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:04.510
And it's hard to tell sometimes
the difference between the two.

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:06.940
Like when you use
one versus another?

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:08.100
So I'll make it real easy.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:09.972
If you're doing
stuff server side,

00:27:09.972 --> 00:27:11.680
you probably want to
use Cloud Datastore.

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:16.650
You're talking from Google Cloud
somewhere else on Google Cloud.

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:18.150
You want that to
be Cloud Datastore.

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:20.608
If, on the other hand, you're
doing a lot with client work,

00:27:20.608 --> 00:27:22.200
you're doing a
lot where you want

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:23.910
to bypass the server
completely and just

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:27.465
have data stored directly from
the client onto a datastore,

00:27:27.465 --> 00:27:28.840
Cloud Firestore
is right for you.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:30.450
Cloud Firestore is
sort of a marriage

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:32.640
of some of our Cloud
Datastore technology

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:36.790
and the expertise of
the Firebase team.

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:40.860
And so because of that, it has
a great web and mobile SDK,

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:45.280
and really works well
with those environments.

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:46.610
So which one should we use?

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:48.085
Well, I'm not
switching to NoSQL,

00:27:48.085 --> 00:27:49.960
because it was written
in SQL, and I'm just--

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.030
I'm not switching to NoSQL.

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:54.160
That would be like
much more than I'd

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:56.970
want to do for an
experiment like this.

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.450
Cloud Spanner is
really overkill,

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.610
and so what I'm going
to do is use Cloud SQL.

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:02.620
So let me drive Cloud
SQL a little bit for you.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:06.440
Here's our SQL machine.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.820
I'm going to go ahead
and create an instance.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:13.130
You'll see it gives me a choice
between MySQL and Postgres.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:15.756
I'm going to choose MySQL.

00:28:15.756 --> 00:28:18.380
You'll see here it gives me some
kind of pre-configured options

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:19.550
to make stuff easier.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:22.610
I'm going to go with
a development server,

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:25.610
and then mess it up.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:26.250
Let me do that.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:30.200
I'm going to call this Tagger.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:30.920
Let's see.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:32.670
And then here's where
I can have some fun.

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:35.200
I can configure the type
of machine and storage.

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:36.210
So I'm going to start.

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.890
Right now, the
initial one is micro.

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:39.306
And you'll notice
over here that I

00:28:39.306 --> 00:28:41.700
have a performance
kind of meter,

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:43.580
how performant this
machine is going to be.

00:28:43.580 --> 00:28:45.413
I'm going go ahead and
change the processor.

00:28:45.413 --> 00:28:47.640
When I change the
processor, you'll

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:49.480
see that I maxed out
network throughput.

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:51.600
So if you want more
network throughput,

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:55.560
add more processors to your
SQL boxes on Cloud SQL.

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.720
If I want better IOPS,
well, I can just--

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:00.550
hold on.

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:02.470
You'll see here
that as I increase

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.926
the number of IOPS, the
disk throughput and IOPS

00:29:04.926 --> 00:29:05.800
and all that goes up.

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:09.190
And as I add more,
eventually, I max it out.

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:11.850
So if you need higher
performance out of your SQL

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:16.030
servers on Cloud SQL,
that's the way to do it.

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:18.375
And then I can also enable
automatic storage increases,

00:29:18.375 --> 00:29:19.000
which is great.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.250
Like if I'm getting
close to my disk,

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:23.470
instead of having
to have a big--

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:26.714
not that being here is
a problem, but if I'm

00:29:26.714 --> 00:29:28.380
worried about that,
I can just enable it

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.130
so that it will grow
as my dataset grows.

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:33.320
So OK, I am going to
cancel out of that.

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.786
And then I'm going to
switch back to presenting.

00:29:41.017 --> 00:29:43.350
All right, so how do I get
my data to include Cloud SQL?

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:44.766
Well, I do a
database dump, right?

00:29:44.766 --> 00:29:46.810
Like I do MySQL dump.

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:49.540
And then I do gsutil and
copy it to Cloud Storage,

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:50.670
of all places.

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:55.210
Once it's in Cloud Storage,
I run sql import sql

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:57.149
to import that file.

00:29:57.149 --> 00:29:59.440
And then after I import the
file, because you shouldn't

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:01.630
leave your SQL
dumps lying around

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:05.220
at a place that could be made
public, you delete the file.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.060
Now-- oh, slight detour.

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:10.090
This pattern of pushing
stuff to Cloud Storage

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:12.620
and then pulling it down
elsewhere in Google Cloud

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:14.736
Platform, it's kind of common.

00:30:14.736 --> 00:30:16.360
And I kind of see
Cloud Storage as sort

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.440
of hub between a lot of
different technologies.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:18.700
You see this.

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:20.700
You have an application
running Cloud Functions.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.990
You put a picture
in Cloud Storage.

00:30:22.990 --> 00:30:26.710
And then manipulate it within
Vision API, or something else.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:29.987
This pattern of being
a hub also applies

00:30:29.987 --> 00:30:31.570
to Cloud Pub/Sub,
which I'm not really

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:33.528
going to show off because
it doesn't make sense

00:30:33.528 --> 00:30:34.230
for this app.

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:37.630
But Cloud Pub/Sub
does for messages what

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:39.360
Cloud Storage does for files.

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:41.440
It basically acts as a
conduit in between all.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:45.440
So it is a messaging bus that
does the publisher/subscriber

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:45.940
model.

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:48.398
You have many publishers or
one publisher, many subscribers

00:30:48.398 --> 00:30:49.330
or one.

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:53.290
And then it'll make sure
that all the subscribers

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:58.180
get all the messages
from all the publishers.

00:30:58.180 --> 00:30:59.220
I'll come back to that.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.520
All right, now, some common
problems with doing Cloud SQL.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:09.970
First one is you do glcoud
sql, and you import a file,

00:31:09.970 --> 00:31:11.400
and you get an error.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:12.820
This is an internal error.

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.937
OK, I didn't think it'd be
an external error, but fine.

00:31:15.937 --> 00:31:17.020
All right, internal error.

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:20.290
And then you dig into it
and you set up verbose mode.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:23.920
And it'll tell you,
guys, guys, this

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:27.160
was a MySQL error, which is
really not that much more

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:28.370
helpful.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.220
And I have a bug filed.

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:32.600
I'm not bitter that
they haven't fixed it.

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:35.330
But how do you figure
out this problem?

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:38.670
So in the Cloud console, there's
this thing called Operations--

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:39.829
a tab called Operations.

00:31:39.829 --> 00:31:41.620
At the top you'll see
that I have an error,

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:43.150
and it even includes
a line number.

00:31:43.150 --> 00:31:45.520
So I can track this down
and maybe see what it is.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:49.337
Turns out that MySQL dump,
if you had super privileges

00:31:49.337 --> 00:31:51.670
locally, and you might have
because you're experimenting

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:53.461
with stuff and you
accidentally upped them,

00:31:53.461 --> 00:31:56.152
like I totally did
in this case, you

00:31:56.152 --> 00:31:58.360
can't do that because we
don't allow super privileges

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:00.780
on the box.

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:03.490
So watch out for that.

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:07.660
All right, so what if you
need something we don't offer?

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:09.730
Like I talked about Cloud SQL.

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:11.110
I talked about our products.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:13.240
But maybe you need
Redis, or maybe you

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:17.320
need Mongo, or
Cassandra, or something.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:18.470
Well, there's two options.

00:32:18.470 --> 00:32:22.390
You could always build it by
hand using Compute Engine,

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:23.380
because it's just a VM.

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:25.160
You can install stuff on it.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:27.940
We also have the thing called
Cloud Marketplace, which

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.170
just recently was renamed.

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:30.326
If you're familiar
with the platform,

00:32:30.326 --> 00:32:31.534
it was called Cloud Launcher.

00:32:31.534 --> 00:32:32.835
It's now Cloud Marketplace.

00:32:32.835 --> 00:32:34.210
And there are
pre-built solutions

00:32:34.210 --> 00:32:36.967
for things like Cassandra,
Redis, Mongo, CouchDB and more.

00:32:36.967 --> 00:32:38.800
Now, it's important to
note that we will not

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.270
manage these for you.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:41.290
We build them.

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:43.990
We like one-click
build these things.

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:46.360
But then managing
them is on you.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:49.840
It won't be the way
our SQL boxes are.

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:51.350
All right, so here we are.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:54.430
We've got our app from a web
application on a single server.

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:56.677
And now we're running
all our state elsewhere.

00:32:56.677 --> 00:32:58.510
And we have all our
code on a single server.

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:01.560
This opens up a whole lot of
possibilities for us, right?

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:05.230
Because this is the
modern, the 12-factor app,

00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:06.490
at least part of it.

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:09.130
Like we have application
code running in one place,

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:12.220
and we don't have
to state anywhere.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:13.720
And so we can start
mucking around

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:16.019
with what we're doing
on the single server.

00:33:16.019 --> 00:33:18.310
So let's talk about some of
our computing options, what

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:20.200
we use to build this stuff.

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.122
So how do we scale this system?

00:33:22.122 --> 00:33:23.830
Now, there are a couple
of different ways

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:26.709
of scaling the system, like
what happens if Tagger becomes

00:33:26.709 --> 00:33:27.500
a breakout success?

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:28.499
It won't happen, I know.

00:33:28.499 --> 00:33:29.230
Fine.

00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:30.170
Won't happen.

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:31.690
But what if it does?

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:33.980
Well, there are a couple of
ways of dealing with this.

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:37.120
The first way would be to use
a completely Compute Engine

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:41.530
solution, which is a combination
of using a managed instance

00:33:41.530 --> 00:33:43.540
group and a network
load balancer.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:45.670
So a managed
instance group, which

00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:48.070
I'll talk about a little
bit more in a second,

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:51.100
is basically a
collection of VMs that

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:54.910
perform the same role of be
pointed at one load balancer.

00:33:54.910 --> 00:33:57.306
And then our load balancers
are load balancers, which

00:33:57.306 --> 00:33:58.430
are pretty straightforward.

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:00.100
They don't kind of
know what they are.

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:01.210
So talk a little bit about them.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.126
Our instance groups, we
could say that they're

00:34:03.126 --> 00:34:04.450
managed or unmanaged.

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:06.100
I chose managed for
that, because one

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:08.141
of the cool things about
a managed instance group

00:34:08.141 --> 00:34:11.169
is you can say things like, all
right, because all the machines

00:34:11.169 --> 00:34:12.960
are exactly the same,
you create a template

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:15.670
and it will create the same
machine over and over again.

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:19.000
You can say, I want processor
utilization to be at 60.

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:21.408
If it goes above 60,
spin up another box.

00:34:21.408 --> 00:34:23.699
And because you have a
template, it can spin up the box

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:24.407
and it's perfect.

00:34:24.407 --> 00:34:26.710
And it perfectly fits
what you're doing.

00:34:26.710 --> 00:34:29.770
You also do unmanaged, where
you can build your own machines

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:31.300
and add them to it.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:33.550
But then they could
be different profiles

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:36.310
of processor and memory
and that sort of thing.

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:39.489
So we can't really
auto-scale or auto-heal them.

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:41.310
So that's how you make
a collection of VMs

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:42.880
that all do the same task.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:45.130
You throw a load
balancer on top of them.

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:48.969
And we have-- the
interface will show you

00:34:48.969 --> 00:34:50.350
three different options.

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:53.800
We'll show you
HTTP, TCP, and UDP.

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:56.179
And you can make any
one of them local--

00:34:56.179 --> 00:34:57.280
sorry, internal only.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.600
So it doesn't get exposed
to the public internet.

00:35:00.600 --> 00:35:04.280
So HTTP is our equivalent
of an L7 load balancer.

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.620
TCP, UDP is the
equivalent of an L4.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:10.650
And then all of them can
be made internal only.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:13.920
So we're at cloud conference
and I have not said Docker.

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:15.180
I have not said container.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:16.263
And I have not serverless.

00:35:16.263 --> 00:35:17.850
So what's going on, right?

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:20.040
I also haven't AI.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:21.550
So we're big data, right?

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:24.060
But I'm going to focus on
container and serverless.

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:28.240
These are two terms
that you typically

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:31.210
want to maybe consider
moving your container,

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:34.774
your computing load to.

00:35:34.774 --> 00:35:36.440
So what are containers,
for those of you

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:37.190
that might not be familiar?

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:39.196
Containers basically
consist of two things.

00:35:39.196 --> 00:35:41.570
And this is-- if there are
container experts in the room,

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:42.260
I'm sorry.

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:44.090
I really oversimplified
it, but I

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:45.950
wanted to make it
really simple for people

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:47.310
who are not familiar with it.

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:49.530
So containers are
made up of two parts.

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:52.340
One, a package file containing
all of the code and runtime

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:55.940
components it takes to process
your app, or your microservice,

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:57.759
or whatever you're running.

00:35:57.759 --> 00:35:59.300
And then you have
an environment that

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:00.747
tricks that package
into thinking

00:36:00.747 --> 00:36:02.330
it's the only machine
that's running--

00:36:02.330 --> 00:36:06.060
it's the only system
running on that machine.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:08.150
Now, this is in
contrast to a VM,

00:36:08.150 --> 00:36:10.910
where you carve off
bits of the machine

00:36:10.910 --> 00:36:13.400
and say, use 16 megs of memory.

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:17.430
And if it only uses 8, well,
then that extra memory is gone.

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:20.270
You can't pull it back
because it's been carved off.

00:36:20.270 --> 00:36:23.570
So doing it this
way, these containers

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:27.589
are all kind of tricked
into using the same kernel.

00:36:27.589 --> 00:36:28.130
I don't know.

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:29.213
Maybe they're not tricked.

00:36:29.213 --> 00:36:31.980
Maybe they're just induced
or encouraged or something.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:35.077
But what that allows them to
do is spin up very, very fast,

00:36:35.077 --> 00:36:37.410
because we don't have the
whole operating system tear up

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:38.480
and tear down.

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:40.550
And we can fit a
lot more of them,

00:36:40.550 --> 00:36:44.660
because unlike a VM, where you
allocate blocks of resource,

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:46.070
these are just processes.

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:49.000
And they use exactly as
much memory and resources

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:50.354
as they need.

00:36:50.354 --> 00:36:52.520
And then the rest is still
free for other containers

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:55.100
to pick up and use.

00:36:55.100 --> 00:36:57.370
So there are two
container solutions

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:00.130
that I like to talk about
it at Google Platform.

00:37:00.130 --> 00:37:02.340
You obviously can spin
up Docker on a VM.

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:03.840
And there's nothing
wrong with that.

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:06.364
And we actually make it
relatively easy to do.

00:37:06.364 --> 00:37:08.530
But the two container
solutions I like to talk about

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:12.340
are Kubernetes Engine,
which is managed Kubernetes.

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:15.114
Anybody here familiar
with Kubernetes?

00:37:15.114 --> 00:37:17.952
OK, that's intimidating.

00:37:17.952 --> 00:37:19.660
So good, you all know
what Kubernetes is.

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:20.660
For those of you
that don't know,

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:22.660
Kubernetes is a container
orchestration system.

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:24.280
When you have one
container, it's

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:25.960
pretty easy to keep track of.

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.155
When you start having
20, 30 containers that

00:37:28.155 --> 00:37:29.530
are all part of
different systems

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:33.160
that all need to be wired up to
different networking solutions,

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:35.530
it becomes challenging to
keep track of them all.

00:37:35.530 --> 00:37:37.990
And so Kubernetes
manages that for you.

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:39.730
That's really
oversimplification.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.390
Now, Kubernetes itself
is a very large bit

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:44.657
of software to manage.

00:37:44.657 --> 00:37:46.990
Kubernetes Engine is probably
the easiest way to run it,

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:48.580
because we'll take
care of building

00:37:48.580 --> 00:37:50.205
all the machines for
you and setting up

00:37:50.205 --> 00:37:52.570
smart defaults for monitoring,
logging, DNS, service

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.946
discovery, and auto-scaling.

00:37:54.946 --> 00:37:57.070
We also have this thing
called App Engine flexible,

00:37:57.070 --> 00:37:58.620
which is much more simple.

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:01.620
You basically create a
custom runtime Docker image.

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:03.522
It's got to serve
up on port 8080.

00:38:03.522 --> 00:38:04.980
And once you do
that, we will scale

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:08.190
it to as little or as much
as you need, starting at one.

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:10.110
So we'll leave it
running all the time.

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:11.730
You have one container
and one service

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:13.200
that's running on one endpoint.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.575
And if you get a lot of traffic,
we'll just scale it out.

00:38:15.575 --> 00:38:17.670
And if you get less traffic,
we'll scale it down.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:19.800
Much more simple,
but it's better

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:21.760
when you have just
a very simple thing.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:24.486
It's the quick and easy fix
for a lot of this stuff.

00:38:24.486 --> 00:38:27.700
All right, so what
is serverless?

00:38:27.700 --> 00:38:29.900
Serverless is a marketing term.

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:30.551
We all know it.

00:38:30.551 --> 00:38:31.550
We're all in technology.

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:33.080
We all know it's
a marketing term,

00:38:33.080 --> 00:38:36.805
because we know there's not some
sort of weird octopus creature

00:38:36.805 --> 00:38:38.180
that these things
are running on.

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:39.054
It's a server, right?

00:38:39.054 --> 00:38:40.479
There's a server somewhere.

00:38:40.479 --> 00:38:42.020
And we know that
serverless is a lie.

00:38:42.020 --> 00:38:45.280
But it describes something.

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:48.520
For you, the user, the
person consuming the service,

00:38:48.520 --> 00:38:50.922
the server has
disappeared, because you

00:38:50.922 --> 00:38:51.880
don't interact with it.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:55.060
You don't ever have to
worry about setting it up,

00:38:55.060 --> 00:38:57.940
or did I provision enough, or
does it have enough memory?

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:00.170
We take care of that for you.

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:01.720
So serverless
describes a service

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:03.520
where you pay as
you go for only what

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:06.361
you use, and you don't have
to be aware of the system it's

00:39:06.361 --> 00:39:06.860
running on.

00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:09.190
And that is actually
a really cool thing,

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:10.600
even if it is a marketing term.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:13.909
All right, so serverless,
we have two products

00:39:13.909 --> 00:39:15.700
that I kind of call
the serverless products

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:16.630
at Google Cloud Platform.

00:39:16.630 --> 00:39:18.700
One is Cloud Functions,
which is function-based.

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:19.910
You write a function.

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:20.860
Send mail.

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:22.720
One function sends email, right?

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:25.580
You then attach them to events.

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.720
So someone writes a
file to Cloud Storage.

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.910
You can attach a Cloud
Function to that and send mail.

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:33.730
You can attach that event
to a Pub/Sub message.

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:35.820
You can attach that
event to a URL call.

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:40.090
So you can actually make URL
calls based on Cloud Functions

00:39:40.090 --> 00:39:42.460
Now, if you need more
than just one function,

00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:44.860
you need to chain a whole
bunch of environment stuff

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:47.110
together and create a
full application, well,

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:50.282
App Engine standard is
probably the way to go there.

00:39:50.282 --> 00:39:52.490
We have a number of runtimes
for App Engine standard,

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:56.946
which we announced today
that we are releasing

00:39:56.946 --> 00:39:58.570
some new ones in the
near future, which

00:39:58.570 --> 00:40:00.380
is really exciting.

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:03.130
But basically, you have to
write in these languages

00:40:03.130 --> 00:40:05.691
in order to use App
Engine standard.

00:40:05.691 --> 00:40:06.940
And there's some restrictions.

00:40:06.940 --> 00:40:12.310
Like if your software is
based on a C executable

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:15.550
that you wrote 10 years ago, you
can't pull that into App Engine

00:40:15.550 --> 00:40:17.450
and have App Engine scale it.

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.750
But if you keep with those
constrained capabilities

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:22.240
and you just write code in
these runtime languages,

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:24.880
we will be incredibly,
incredibly rapidly

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:26.560
scale your application.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:27.280
I have a demo--

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:28.363
I'm not going to show it--

00:40:28.363 --> 00:40:30.520
where I take an App
Engine app that is cold,

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:33.730
that hasn't been hit in a day.

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:38.680
And I scale it from 0 to
8,500 queries per second

00:40:38.680 --> 00:40:40.820
in less than five minutes.

00:40:40.820 --> 00:40:44.990
So 8,500 QPS is pretty high,
but what does it really mean?

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:47.650
8,500 QPS is about 30
million requests an hour,

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:50.980
or what Wikipedia does
on an average hour.

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:54.310
So on App Engine, you
can scale from nothing

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:57.640
to a Wikipedia
level traffic site

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:04.150
in less than five minutes, which
is really, really cool and fun.

00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:07.360
So the question is, so this
Tagger service, this thing,

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:09.250
should I switch this
over to one of those?

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:13.660
And my answer is, well,
it depends, right?

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:15.460
Typical weasel IT answer.

00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:19.600
It depends, because if--

00:41:19.600 --> 00:41:20.904
probably not, right?

00:41:20.904 --> 00:41:22.570
It's just a simple
app that I'm running.

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:24.850
It's probably like a legacy
app that I've rolled over

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:25.927
and I need to keep going.

00:41:25.927 --> 00:41:27.760
I don't know that I
would move it off of VM.

00:41:27.760 --> 00:41:29.840
It's running fine,
just leave it on a VM.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:31.810
But let's say I want
growth in this app.

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:34.130
Let's say I want it to
go bigger and better.

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:41.975
Then maybe I would push it to
containers and have it run with

00:41:41.975 --> 00:41:44.350
a whole bunch of other similar
services so I get the most

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:48.160
resources out of my
hardware that I'm--

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:50.380
or the virtual hardware
that I'm pulling down.

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:55.150
Also, if I have this app that's
not very frequently used,

00:41:55.150 --> 00:41:56.864
running a VM all
the time may also

00:41:56.864 --> 00:41:58.780
be something that would
be too much of a cost.

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:00.190
So I might switch it
over to serverless

00:42:00.190 --> 00:42:02.148
and take advantage of
the fact that serverless,

00:42:02.148 --> 00:42:03.430
you only pay for what's used.

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:05.013
It would depend on
how much it's used.

00:42:05.013 --> 00:42:07.219
So my answer is depends,
but for this app,

00:42:07.219 --> 00:42:09.260
I don't see it lighting
up anytime in the future,

00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:12.550
so I would probably
leave it on the VM.

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:15.250
So conclusions.

00:42:15.250 --> 00:42:17.040
One-- so we started here.

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:18.765
Let me just kind of
recap what we did.

00:42:18.765 --> 00:42:19.890
We had a single server app.

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:24.214
We moved it over to this sort
of configuration, single server,

00:42:24.214 --> 00:42:26.130
which opened up a whole
bunch of possibilities

00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:27.713
to us of maybe
switching to containers

00:42:27.713 --> 00:42:30.430
or maybe switching
to serverless.

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:32.460
And we left our
images and our data

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:34.800
elsewhere, which was great.

